==========================================
NETWORKING DAY 11 NOTES — tcpdump Part 2
Date: 09-10-2026
==========================================

WHAT I LEARNED TODAY:
- tcpdump filters (BPF syntax) decide WHICH packets get captured, so output isn't a firehose
- port <n>, tcp / udp / icmp, and host <ip> are the three building blocks
- src and dst narrow a filter to one direction only
- and / or / not combine filters; quote multi-word filters so the shell leaves them alone
- 'not port 22' is the classic filter to hide your own SSH session while capturing
- A filter used with -w only SAVES matching packets; a filter used with -r filters an existing file
- TCP flags in output: [S] SYN, [S.] SYN-ACK, [.] ACK, [P.] data, [F.] FIN, [R] reset

COMMANDS I PRACTICED:
[ ] tcpdump -i lo -n port 8080
[ ] tcpdump -i lo -n icmp
[ ] tcpdump -i eth0 -n host / dst host / src host
[ ] tcpdump -i lo -n 'tcp and port 8080' -A
[ ] tcpdump -i eth0 -n 'not port 22'  /  'icmp or port 53'
[ ] tcpdump -w with a filter, then -r with a second filter

paste your real output here :
$ sudo tcpdump -i lo -n port 8080 -c 10
> 

$ sudo tcpdump -i eth0 -n dst host 1.1.1.1 -c 4
> 

$ sudo tcpdump -i lo -n 'tcp and port 8080' -c 10 -A
> 


